## Solution

**Answer:** The limit is $\dfrac{|G|}{|Z(G)|}$, where $Z(G)$ is the center of $G$.

### Setup

Two tuples $(a_0,\dots,a_n)$ and $(b_0,\dots,b_n)\in G^{n+1}$ define the same function iff
$$a_0\,x_1\,a_1\,x_2\cdots x_n\,a_n \;=\; b_0\,x_1\,b_1\,x_2\cdots x_n\,b_n \quad\text{for all } x_1,\dots,x_n\in G. \tag{$\ast$}$$

Define an action of $Z(G)^n$ on $G^{n+1}$: for $(z_1,\dots,z_n)\in Z(G)^n$,
$$(a_0,a_1,\dots,a_n)\;\mapsto\;\bigl(a_0 z_1,\; z_1^{-1}a_1 z_2,\; \dots,\; z_{n-1}^{-1}a_{n-1}z_n,\; z_n^{-1}a_n\bigr).$$

Since each $z_i$ is central, $z_i\,x_i\,z_i^{-1}=x_i$, so every element of an orbit yields the **same** function. Hence $t_n(G)$ equals the number of orbits times... — we will show the orbits are **exactly** the equivalence classes and that the action is **free**.

### The action is free

Suppose $(z_1,\dots,z_n)$ fixes $(a_0,\dots,a_n)$. From $a_0 z_1=a_0$ we get $z_1=e$. From $z_n^{-1}a_n=a_n$ we get $z_n=e$. For $1\le i\le n-1$, centrality gives $z_i^{-1}a_i z_{i+1}=a_i z_i^{-1}z_{i+1}=a_i$, so $z_{i+1}=z_i$. Thus $z_1=\cdots=z_n=e$. The stabilizer is trivial, so **every orbit has size $|Z(G)|^n$**.

### Orbits = equivalence classes (by induction on $n$)

Sufficiency was noted above. For necessity, assume $(\ast)$. Setting $x_2=\cdots=x_n=e$ gives
$$a_0\,x_1\,(a_1\cdots a_n)=b_0\,x_1\,(b_1\cdots b_n)\quad\forall x_1.$$
Setting $x_1=e$ yields $a_0(a_1\cdots a_n)=b_0(b_1\cdots b_n)$. Substituting back and cancelling $a_0$ on the left (valid since $a_0$ is a fixed left factor) gives
$$x_1\,\alpha = x_1\,\alpha\,\beta^{-1}x_1^{-1}\cdot x_1\,\beta \;\Rightarrow\; \alpha\beta^{-1}x_1=x_1\,\alpha\beta^{-1}\quad\forall x_1,$$
where $\alpha=a_1\cdots a_n$, $\beta=b_1\cdots b_n$. Hence $z_1:=\alpha\beta^{-1}\in Z(G)$, and $b_0=a_0 z_1$.

Using centrality of $z_1$, rewrite the right side of $(\ast)$:
$$b_0\,x_1\,b_1\cdots = a_0 z_1\,x_1\,b_1\cdots = a_0\,x_1\,(z_1 b_1)\,x_2\,b_2\cdots.$$
Setting $x_1=e$ in $(\ast)$ now gives
$$a_1\,x_2\,a_2\cdots x_n a_n = (z_1 b_1)\,x_2\,b_2\cdots x_n b_n \quad\forall\, x_2,\dots,x_n.$$
This is the same problem with $n-1$ variables. By the inductive hypothesis, there exist $z_2,\dots,z_n\in Z(G)$ with
$$z_1 b_1 = a_1 z_2,\quad b_i=z_i^{-1}a_i z_{i+1}\;(2\le i\le n-1),\quad b_n=z_n^{-1}a_n.$$
Together with $b_0=a_0 z_1$ this is exactly the orbit condition. The base case $n=0$ is trivial. $\square$

### Counting

Since equivalence classes are exactly the orbits and each orbit has size $|Z(G)|^n$,
$$t_n(G)=\frac{|G|^{n+1}}{|Z(G)|^n}.$$

### The limit

$$t_n(G)^{1/n}=\frac{|G|^{(n+1)/n}}{|Z(G)|}=\frac{|G|^{1+1/n}}{|Z(G)|}\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\;\frac{|G|}{|Z(G)|}.$$

**Sanity checks.** If $G$ is abelian, $Z(G)=G$ and $f=a_0a_1\cdots a_n\cdot x_1\cdots x_n$ depends only on the product $a_0\cdots a_n\in G$, so $t_n(G)=|G|$ and $|G|^{1/n}\to 1=|G|/|G|$. ✓ If $Z(G)=\{e\}$ (e.g. $G=S_3$), then $t_n(G)=|G|^{n+1}$ and the limit is $|G|$. ✓

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}t_n(G)^{1/n}=\frac{|G|}{|Z(G)|}}$$

